@meta
id: 27-07
title: Two buffers, one setting
chapter: 27
type: lesson
skills: set-local, buffer-switch
par: 47
minutes: 6
vscode: per-language settings JSON, edited in a third tab
open: orders.sql order.provider.ts

@brief
Two files, two indent conventions. The SQL wants four spaces inside a
statement; the TypeScript wants two, as it always has.

Do it with `:set shiftwidth=4` and you will fix the SQL and break the
TypeScript, because `:set` on a buffer-local option sets two things at
once: this buffer's value, and the value every buffer you open from now on
inherits. The damage does not show up until you switch buffers, which is
what makes it hard to spot.

`:setlocal` sets only this buffer. That is the one you want here.

Indent the two continuation lines in `orders.sql` by four, switch to
`order.provider.ts`, and indent its two fields by two. Write both.

@keys
| `:setlocal shiftwidth=4` | set it for this buffer only |
| `:set shiftwidth=4` | set it here *and* for everything opened later |
| `:setglobal shiftwidth=4` | set the inherited value, leave this buffer alone |
| `:setlocal shiftwidth?` | this buffer's value |
| `:setglobal shiftwidth?` | the value new buffers will get |

@hints
You only need to change the option once. The question is which command
you change it with, and whether the second buffer inherits it.
--
`:setlocal` stops at this buffer's edge. `:set` does not.
--
`:setlocal shiftwidth=4` `Enter`, `2G2>>`, `:bn` `Enter`, `2G2>>`,
`:wqa` `Enter`.

@solution
    :setlocal shiftwidth=4<Enter>  2G  2>>
    :bn<Enter>  2G  2>>  :wqa<Enter>

Forty-two keystrokes. Swap that first command for `:set shiftwidth=4` and
everything else is identical — and the TypeScript comes out with four
spaces.

Every option in vim is one of three kinds:

**Global.** One value for the whole session. `ignorecase`, `hlsearch`,
`laststatus`, `history`. `:setlocal` on one of these is just `:set`.

**Buffer-local.** One value per file. `shiftwidth`, `expandtab`,
`textwidth`, `formatoptions`, `filetype`, `comments`, `spelllang`.
Anything about the content.

**Window-local.** One value per window. `number`, `wrap`, `list`,
`cursorline`, `foldmethod`. Anything about the view. Split a file in two
and `:setlocal number` in the top window: the bottom window keeps its old
setting, on the same buffer. That surprises people, and it is the right
behaviour — you can have one window showing line numbers and another not,
because the numbers are a property of looking, not of the file.

The rule for the two local kinds is the same: `:set` writes both the local
value and the global one, `:setlocal` writes only the local, `:setglobal`
only the global. Interactively you nearly always mean `:setlocal`. In a
config file you nearly always mean `:set`, because there is no buffer worth
talking about yet — except inside a filetype plugin, where `:setlocal` is
the only correct answer. That is the next lesson.

@start orders.sql
UPDATE orders
SET status = 'CANCELLED',
cancellation_reason = 'REFUND_WINDOW_EXPIRED'
WHERE id = ?;

@expected orders.sql
UPDATE orders
    SET status = 'CANCELLED',
    cancellation_reason = 'REFUND_WINDOW_EXPIRED'
WHERE id = ?;

@start order.provider.ts
export const refundWindow = {
days: 14,
graceHours: 12,
};

@expected order.provider.ts
export const refundWindow = {
  days: 14,
  graceHours: 12,
};
